WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.690
You say you are open to every view — until someone sits across from you who wants your view erased altogether.

2
00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:13.540
At that point patience stops being generosity and starts looking like a door left open to whoever means you harm.

3
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:20.390
So where exactly does tolerance end: at the words, at the harm, or at the moment you can no longer afford it?

4
00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.920
Picture a residents' meeting in an apartment block.

5
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.290
The dullest hour and a half of the year: service charges, roof insulation, lift maintenance.

6
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:47.060
Then someone takes the floor and says something nobody saw coming: I don't think certain people should be living in this building.

7
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:49.590
A silence falls.

8
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:55.000
And everyone in that room, in that instant, feels two things at the same time.

9
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.250
The first is that this man has a right to speak as well; this is his home too.

10
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:05.180
The second is that this man has just put other people's right to be here up for discussion.

11
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.710
In other words, the idea we are being asked to bear is an idea that proposes doing away with bearing anything at all.

12
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:20.320
This is how the most uncomfortable question of an entire tradition of thought seeps into ordinary life, slipping in between the agenda items on roof insulation.

13
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.170
How far does tolerance go?

14
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.140
Is it to be extended even to the one who wants to destroy it?

15
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:35.430
Before we ask that, we need to scratch at the word itself, because in Turkish the word hoşgörü is a recent one, and slightly misleading.

16
00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:41.480
Before it, people said müsamaha, they said tahammül, they said tolerans.

17
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:50.570
Its counterpart in Western languages comes from the Latin verb tolerare, and its meaning is precisely this: to carry, to shoulder, to endure.

18
00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.540
So at the root of the word there is no smile; there is weight.

19
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.030
Tolerance is not finding something agreeable.

20
00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.480
It is carrying something you do not find agreeable, in spite of that.

21
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.410
This distinction looks like a small matter of language, but everything hangs on it.

22
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:12.500
Because the thing tolerance is most often confused with is indifference.

23
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:20.950
If I have no curiosity at all about what my neighbour eats, which team he supports, which god he believes in, then looking past it costs me nothing.

24
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.280
There is no virtue there, only disinterest.

25
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:30.010
Tolerance, on the other hand, can only stand on top of an objection.

26
00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:39.580
If I find something wrong, even harmful, and in spite of that I hold back from banning it, from silencing it, from punishing it, then I really am carrying a weight.

27
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.470
Which means tolerance is not the problem of the indifferent person but of the person with convictions.

28
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.120
Someone who believes in nothing has nothing to tolerate either.

29
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:55.010
And beneath that weight there is also a hidden insult, which Goethe stated as plainly as anyone.

30
00:02:55.010 --> 00:03:07.460
In the notebooks where he gathered his reflections and maxims, he writes that tolerance ought really to be a temporary attitude; it must lead to acceptance, to recognition, because merely to put up with someone, he says,

31
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.150
is to insult them.

32
00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.440
A very harsh sentence, but there is something right in it.

33
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:20.170
To tell a person "I can endure your existence" is not to tell them they are your equal.

34
00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:25.100
It is to speak from somewhere above, from a position that holds the power to permit.

35
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:31.790
That is why tolerance is at once one of the greatest achievements in human history and one of its most arrogant words.

36
00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.120
So now we have seen two things at once.

37
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.770
Tolerance is not indifference; it is a burden.

38
00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:43.860
And the side carrying that burden holds a certain power over what it carries.

39
00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.030
Which means the real question changes shape.

40
00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.160
The question is no longer how patient a good person ought to be.

41
00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:59.610
The question is this: what will a society that holds this power do about an idea that has written the abolition of that society into its programme?

42
00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:05.340
If it endures, there may be nobody left to do the enduring once the enduring is over.

43
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:11.710
If it refuses to endure, then at the very moment of refusal it begins to resemble the thing it refused.

44
00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:18.240
This deadlock is not one person's failure of character; it is a crack in the structure of tolerance itself.

45
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:25.250
And the interesting part is this: the first people to notice the crack were not tolerance's enemies but its greatest defenders.

46
00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:31.780
Almost every one of the men who established tolerance as a principle drew a line at the edge of the principle with his own hand.

47
00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.710
Looking at where they drew that line will tell us something about the apartment meeting as well.

48
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:46.120
In 1685, the French king Louis the Fourteenth revoked the arrangement that had given the country's Protestants a limited guarantee for roughly a century.

49
00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:57.130
The result was one of the largest refugee waves Europe had ever seen; hundreds of thousands of people left their homes, their workshops and their graveyards and fled to Holland, to England, to Prussia.

50
00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:05.420
At that moment there was living in Holland an English physician and philosopher who had himself left his country under political suspicion: John Locke.

51
00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:12.430
In Amsterdam, at the harbour, in the churchyards, he saw that exhausted French-speaking crowd.

52
00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.480
And he wrote a letter.

53
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:24.530
Under its Latin title Epistola de Tolerantia — in English, A Letter Concerning Toleration — it was printed in 1689 without the author's name on it.

54
00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:31.060
Locke's real move in that text was not, as many assume, to say that people should be nicer to one another.

55
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:38.870
He said something far colder and far more destructive: force simply does not work in matters of religion, as a technical matter.

56
00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:46.840
Because the state has prison at its disposal, it has fines, it has exile, it has the sword; all of these change behaviour.

57
00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:52.410
But faith does not reside in behaviour, it resides in the inner conviction of the mind.

58
00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:58.460
You can make a person say any sentence you like under torture; you cannot make him believe that sentence.

59
00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:02.430
Religious coercion is therefore not only cruel but futile.

60
00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:09.600
In this domain, the state cannot by its very structure reach the end it wants with the instrument it has picked up.

61
00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.490
This was a very powerful argument, one that tied tolerance not to compassion but to reason.

62
00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:21.060
And as soon as Locke had built it, he marked out its limits with his own hand.

63
00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.030
Towards the end of the letter he lists a few exceptions.

64
00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:37.000
Those who do not believe in God cannot be tolerated, he says, because in his view the binding force of an oath rests on a divine sanction, and society cannot trust the word of a man who does not recognise it.

65
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:46.890
Another exception covers those who have given their allegiance to the political authority of another country; here Locke has in mind, indirectly, the Catholics of his own day,

66
00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:54.860
and as an explicit example he mentions a Muslim whom he takes to be bound in obedience to the mufti at Constantinople, as he puts it.

67
00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:02.670
Neither of these exceptions has anything to be said for it to modern eyes; both are far too heavily loaded with the political fears of the period.

68
00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:07.600
But the third exception is of an altogether different kind, and it is strikingly modern.

69
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:14.130
Locke says that a doctrine which refuses to grant toleration to others has no right to demand toleration for itself.

70
00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:21.460
Look at where that sentence sits: inside the founding text of tolerance, placed there as tolerance's own limit.

71
00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:30.230
Which means the answer to our question comes out of the hand of the first man to ask it seriously, as a marginal note, as early as the seventeenth century.

72
00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:35.000
Tolerance is conceived here not as a one-sided gift but as a mutual agreement.

73
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:39.930
Everyone who sits down at the table promises to protect the table itself.

74
00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:50.140
About 170 years later, in 1859, John Stuart Mill took up the same question, this time by way of thought and speech rather than religion.

75
00:07:50.140 --> 00:08:00.510
The criterion he set out in his book On Liberty is still at the centre of the argument today: society acquires the right to interfere with a person's action only when that action harms others.

76
00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:04.640
Harming oneself is not, on its own, sufficient grounds.

77
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.250
And when it comes to speech, Mill cuts this principle at a subtle point.

78
00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:19.140
The famous example in the book is this: the opinion that corn dealers starve the poor is protected when it is written in a newspaper, because there it is a claim and can be argued with.

79
00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:28.710
The same sentence, shouted in front of an angry crowd gathered at the corn dealer's door with torches in hand, is no longer an opinion; it is the fuse of an action.

80
00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.040
Mill's line falls in a very important place.

81
00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:36.810
The line lies not in the content of the idea but in what the speech does.

82
00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:40.940
How much you dislike a thought can never be grounds for banning it.

83
00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:48.430
But if a piece of speech has left the realm of argument and entered directly into the mechanics of a harm, it has stepped outside what is protected.

84
00:08:48.430 --> 00:09:00.720
Put Locke's criterion of reciprocity next to Mill's criterion of harm and you have a rough but workable scale: every idea that agrees to stay at the table is protected; the hand that moves to overturn the table is not.

85
00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.170
This debate is often told as something Europe invented in the seventeenth century.

86
00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:12.180
But the experience of living together is much older than that, and part of that experience belongs to this part of the world.

87
00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:21.910
One of the shortest chapters of the Qur'an, the surah al-Kafirun, closes with a final sentence of refusal and of separation: lakum dinukum wa liya din.

88
00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:25.560
To you your religion, and to me mine.

89
00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.730
The tone of that sentence is remarkable.

90
00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:41.020
It does not propose a compromise, a common ground, a synthesis; on the contrary, it accepts the difference in all its sharpness, and precisely from there it draws out the possibility of living side by side.

91
00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:49.790
Elsewhere in the same text, in verse 256 of the surah al-Baqarah, there is a more direct statement: there is no compulsion in religion.

92
00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:55.200
What that verse means has been one of the subjects of debate in Islamic thought for centuries.

93
00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:06.050
But from the standpoint of Turkish intellectual history, an important line of interpretation runs through Abu Mansur al-Maturidi, who lived in Samarkand and died in 944.

94
00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:11.460
Maturidi's view of faith can be summarised like this: faith is the assent of the heart.

95
00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:17.510
The answer to the question of where it is located is therefore not what the tongue says or what the body does.

96
00:10:17.510 --> 00:10:26.920
The political consequence of that theological point is immediate: an assent obtained by the sword is not faith, because the sword cannot reach the place where faith resides.

97
00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:35.690
The argument Locke built in the seventeenth century by saying coercion is futile had a very close relative built in the tenth century in Transoxiana,

98
00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:40.460
inside a completely different language and a completely different set of concerns.

99
00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:44.590
Alongside the idea there is also a practice.

100
00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:54.640
Under the order established by Ottoman administration after 1453, different religious communities lived with their own internal law, their own courts, their own schools.

101
00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:04.530
In 1454 Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror re-established the Greek Orthodox patriarchate in Istanbul and granted the patriarch broad authority over his community.

102
00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:14.740
In 1463, in Bosnia, in the charter he issued to the Franciscan friars, he had it written that their churches would not be touched and that the friars would be free to come and go.

103
00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:23.830
For centuries, during a period when Europe was tearing itself apart in religious wars, this order kept multilingual and multireligious crowds in the same cities.

104
00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:28.600
But it would be wrong to describe that experience as something other than what it was.

105
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.410
This order was not equality; it was hierarchy.

106
00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:43.260
Non-Muslim subjects paid the cizye, the poll tax; their testimony in court was weighed differently; they lived under a series of restrictions ranging from dress to the permitted height of a building.

107
00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:48.990
Legal equality was not even set out as a goal until the Reform Edict of 1856.

108
00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:56.160
What existed here, in other words, was not equal citizenship in the modern sense but the administration of difference.

109
00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:06.690
In the well-known lines attributed to Yunus Emre, it is said that whoever does not look upon the 72 nations with the same eye is, even if he is a learned teacher, in truth a rebel against the divine;

110
00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:11.620
that is the high standard of a mystical ethic, not the description of a legal order.

111
00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:18.630
And here is the truly interesting point: this order, too, had a very clear line.

112
00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:24.040
A different faith, a different language, a different form of worship were all permitted.

113
00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:28.330
The one thing that was not permitted was raising a hand against the order itself.

114
00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:32.780
Rebellion fell outside the frame, and the penalty for it was heavy.

115
00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:38.350
Which is to say that no arrangement for living together that we meet in history has ever been unlimited.

116
00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.880
Every one of them has a threshold.

117
00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:47.730
Locke's threshold was reciprocity, Mill's threshold was harm, the Ottoman threshold was the order itself.

118
00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:51.060
The question has never been whether there should be a limit.

119
00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:56.150
The question is always this: where will the line be drawn, and who will draw it?

120
00:12:56.150 --> 00:13:03.960
The man who asked that question with its full weight was an Austrian philosopher who had fled Europe in the middle of a world war and was teaching in New Zealand.

121
00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:15.290
In a note to the seventh chapter of his book The Open Society and Its Enemies, published in 1945, Karl Popper wrote the most quoted paragraph anyone has written on the subject.

122
00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:20.220
Unlimited tolerance, he says, leads to the disappearance of tolerance.

123
00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:31.710
If we extend it without limit even to those who are intolerant of tolerance, we end up unable to defend the tolerant society and, along with it, tolerance itself; we will have allowed it to be destroyed.

124
00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:34.400
Popper was not saying this in a vacuum.

125
00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:44.290
Right in front of his eyes was the wreckage left by a movement that had come to power using every freedom a constitution granted it, and that had abolished that constitution the moment it arrived.

126
00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:50.660
For him the paradox of tolerance was not an academic puzzle but a description of what had just been lived through.

127
00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:56.870
But most of the people who quote Popper skip the rest of that same note, and the whole subtlety is in the rest of it.

128
00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:04.200
Popper writes explicitly that it does not follow from this that we should always suppress the expression of intolerant philosophies.

129
00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:12.010
So long as we can meet them with rational argument and keep them in check through public opinion, he says, suppression would be most unwise.

130
00:14:12.010 --> 00:14:21.100
The case in which we reserve the right is a much narrower one: if the other side refuses to meet us at the level of reason, if it forbids all argument from the outset,

131
00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:29.710
if it teaches its own followers to answer arguments not with arguments but with fists and with guns, then the situation changes.

132
00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:38.160
Popper's criterion, in other words, is not the content of the idea but whether the idea accepts entering into argument at all.

133
00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:44.050
It is looking, remarkably, at the same place as Locke's reciprocity and Mill's line of harm.

134
00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:52.340
In 1971, in A Theory of Justice, John Rawls took up the same problem in colder legal language and arrived somewhere similar:

135
00:14:52.340 --> 00:15:01.110
a tolerant community should not give up tolerating the intolerant unless it sincerely and with reason believes that its own security is genuinely in danger.

136
00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:07.000
The qualifiers there — genuinely, and with reason — are the words carrying all the weight.

137
00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:12.410
This idea also has an institutional counterpart, and it is called militant democracy.

138
00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:24.700
The term was coined in 1937 by the jurist Karl Loewenstein, himself another German refugee: democracy must possess legal instruments with which to defend itself against those who want to destroy it.

139
00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:29.630
After the war this thought went directly into constitutional text.

140
00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:41.440
Article 21 of the German Basic Law states that parties which, by their aims, seek to abolish the free democratic basic order are unconstitutional, and it leaves the power to make that ruling in one place only:

141
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.810
the Constitutional Court.

142
00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:52.740
That power has in fact been used twice: a far-right party was banned in 1952, and the communist party in 1956.

143
00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.710
The most instructive case, though, is the one that was not banned.

144
00:15:56.710 --> 00:16:01.800
In 2017 the same court rejected an application to ban a far-right party.

145
00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:10.410
The reasoning was not that it found the party's aims innocent; the court stated plainly that those aims were incompatible with the constitutional order.

146
00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:15.820
The reason for the rejection was this: the party had no potential to achieve them.

147
00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:21.230
The danger, in other words, did not carry enough weight to bring the institutions down.

148
00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:27.280
That ruling is almost a word-for-word application of the sentence of Popper's that everybody skips.

149
00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:33.650
We are tolerating the intolerant, because we can deal with them by arguing and at the ballot box.

150
00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:37.780
And here we arrive at the most dangerous link in the whole chain.

151
00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:43.510
The very power offered as the solution to the paradox is also the power most open to abuse.

152
00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:50.680
Because every government is structurally inclined to describe the opposition in front of it as people who want to destroy the order.

153
00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:59.290
In the hands of someone who picks up this justification, the paradox of tolerance turns very easily into a pretext for abolishing tolerance.

154
00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:04.060
That is exactly why the criterion has to be procedural rather than emotional.

155
00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:11.070
"I find this idea dangerous" is not a criterion; anyone can say that about any idea.

156
00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:22.080
The criterion must be concrete and open to outside scrutiny: is there a call to violence, is there a programme for abolishing elections, is there a declaration that the courts will not be recognised,

157
00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:26.850
is there an aim of placing the other side's existence outside the protection of law?

158
00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:35.780
When it is not kept this narrow, a democracy defending itself and an authority that merely says it is defending itself become impossible to tell apart.

159
00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:41.190
And where all of this seems very remote, the same matter comes to us in its everyday form.

160
00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:53.160
A social media platform deciding which content to remove, a university arguing about whom to invite as a speaker, a parents' meeting at a school boiling over; the same structure is present in all of them.

161
00:17:53.160 --> 00:18:00.810
And in all of them there is the risk of falling into one of the same two errors: either putting up with everything and letting the table be overturned,

162
00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:07.340
or declaring everything we cannot put up with to be "intolerant anyway" and shrinking the table with our own hands.

163
00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:14.990
If we go back to the residents' meeting we started with, the answer this tradition gives is not "be patient" and not "silence him".

164
00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:20.560
The answer is closer to this: tolerance is not a feeling, it is a contract.

165
00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.810
There is only one condition for sitting at the table, and that is recognising the table.

166
00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:34.740
Finding your neighbour's opinion ugly is not enough to throw him out; arguing with him, trying to persuade him, outvoting him — that is what these are for.

167
00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:45.590
But if that opinion aims to abolish other people's right to be at the table, and if it has moved to do so not through argument but through actual force, then it is not we who are withdrawing tolerance.

168
00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:50.040
The one leaving the table is the side that refuses to recognise it.

169
00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.570
This is not a comforting answer.

170
00:18:52.570 --> 00:19:02.140
Because we have to judge where that line falls each time anew, inside the concrete case, with no exact ruler in our hands; and every judgement carries a margin of error.

171
00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:08.350
Perhaps that is the real legacy of tolerance: it gives us no peace of mind, it gives us a duty.

172
00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:14.400
The duty of continuing not to resemble the thing we are defending ourselves against while we defend ourselves.

173
00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:24.610
And the duty of building institutions, building courts, writing procedures, so that the authority to draw that line is not left in the hands of whoever would most like to abuse it.

174
00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:35.620
For as long as tolerance remains the virtue of well-meaning people, it is fragile; it only comes to mean something when it is turned into rules that will stand up even in the hands of people who mean harm.

175
00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:39.910
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

176
00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:43.560
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
